g them back from the cache rather than treating them as valid, though I need to be careful since some English variant locales might legitimately share strings with the source.\n\nSince CANVAS_LOCALES includes en-AU, en-GB, and en-CA, identical values could be entirely valid there for certain words, so a blanket \"match equals failure\" rule would misfire. I'm leaning toward a more careful approach: store suspicious matches with metadata so they can be re-verified on a later run rather than immediately rejecting or blindly caching them.\n\nRather than change the cache format itself, which would break other consumers assuming flat string values, I think it's better to add a validation step after translation completes: flag entries where the translated value matches the source (excluding English variants), skip caching those so they retry automatically on future runs, without failing the run outright since translation is only invoked when triggered.\n\nI also realize the drift-check script would then catch missing keys and fail CI, which is actually a good side effect for surfacing this failure mode. And I need to think about what to do with already-committed cache files that may already contain these bad passthrough values from past failed runs, like the nl.json file currently open.\n\nI'm planning to add a shared helper function that detects suspected untranslated passthroughs by comparing trimmed, case-insensitive source and translated strings, while excluding English-family locales from that check.\n\nThen I want to wire this into the caching flow: auditing existing cache entries before checking missing keys so passthroughs get stripped and retried, and skipping cache merges for newly translated passthroughs while logging a warning so they retry next run. I'll also add tests covering this behavior, and I don't think the drift-check script needs changes since missing keys will naturally surface.\n\nI'm reasoning that automatically pruning suspect cache entries on future runs makes sense—since if AI translation fails again the key just stays missing, forcing a proper investigation via check-drift rather than silently masking the issue. A separate audit-only script feels like overkill, so it's simpler to just embed this logic in runI18nTranslationCli, and I should check whether the English-variant locale list already exists somewhere like docs/api-translation.ts before defining it myself.\n\nI want to see if there's a similar pattern needed in tools/translation-adapters, so I'm checking CANVAS_LOCALES for the full locale list and its code structure."},"id":"ef825cea-1e9b-4622-9aa1-f6c2c19ec45f","timestamp":"2026-08-31T17:35:18.248Z","parentId":"85e09b3d-017b-4f8e-9424-d1919645df65"}
